Urinary incontinence
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Involuntary urine leakage, Leakage with physical exertion, Sudden uncontrollable urge to void

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-045
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of mixed urinary incontinence causing severe perineal skin excoriation, pain, and psychological distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a complete loss of bladder control over the last 48 hours in an elderly patient, resulting in severe, painful urine burns on the perineum and vulva, preventing her from walking. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate for an acute urinary tract infection driving the sudden exacerbation, provide immediate local wound care for the excoriation, and establish a short-term continence management plan before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am constantly leaking urine, I cannot stop it, and the skin down there is so raw and burning I cannot even put underwear on.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of mixed urinary incontinence negatively impacting her quality of life, including leakage of urine synchronous with coughing or physical exertion (stress incontinence), and a sudden, intense, uncontrollable urge to void followed by involuntary leakage (urge incontinence). Over the last 48 hours, the frequency has drastically increased (exceeding 15 times daily) with continuous dribbling of urine. This constant exposure has resulted in severe, painful perineal excoriation. She reports new-onset dysuria and foul-smelling urine. She avoids all physical activities due to fear of leakage and severe pain from the chafing.
Functional Status: Immobile; sitting on a towel without undergarments due to severe skin pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of worsening skin breakdown and social isolation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is highly distressed, embarrassed, and uncomfortable.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft with mild suprapubic tenderness to deep palpation; no costovertebral angle tenderness. Genitourinary inspection reveals severe, widespread erythema, maceration, and superficial skin breakdown (excoriation) across the vulva, perineum, and inner thighs, consistent with acute urine burn/incontinence-associated dermatitis. Point-of-care urinalysis is strongly positive for nitrites, leukocytes (3+), and blood, confirming an acute urinary tract infection.
Environmental Observations: House smells strongly of urine; multiple soaked pads in the bathroom bin.
Medication Reconciliation: No active bladder medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Urinary Tract Infection exacerbating chronic Mixed Urinary Incontinence, complicated by severe Incontinence-Associated Dermatitis - Status: Acute flare
Differentials:

Acute Overflow Incontinence (Urinary Retention) - Ruled out as the primary acute issue; the bladder is not palpably distended, and the urinalysis points definitively to an acute infectious exacerbation.

Vulvovaginal Candidiasis - While a secondary fungal infection may develop in the macerated skin, the primary acute driver of the severe burning is the chemical irritation from constant infected urine.

Fistula - Unlikely given the sudden acute escalation over 48 hours alongside signs of an acute UTI, rather than a continuous post-surgical leak.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of mixed urinary incontinence directly driven by a new urinary tract infection, complicated by painful incontinence-associated dermatitis. The objective clinical evidence of a positive urinalysis and severe perineal skin breakdown confirms the diagnosis. The severity of the skin pain, causing functional immobility and profound distress, necessitated urgent after-hours assessment. Delaying evaluation until the in-hours period would have allowed the UTI to progress and caused unmanageable, excruciating pain from the worsening chemical burns on the perineal skin.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Trimethoprim 300mg daily for 5 days to immediately treat the underlying urinary tract infection. Cleaned the excoriated perineal area gently with saline and applied a thick, robust layer of zinc oxide barrier cream (supplied from GP kit) to instantly protect the raw skin from further urine contact. Provided a supply of highly absorbent, medical-grade incontinence pull-ups.
Positive Behavioral Change: To allow the raw skin to heal, apply the thick zinc barrier cream precisely like icing a cake every single time you change your pad; seeing the cream physically block the urine will reinforce this essential skin-care habit. To help flush the bacteria from your bladder, commit to drinking one large glass of water every two hours during the day; noticing your urine become clear and less irritating will validate this hydration strategy.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a continence nurse specialist for long-term management.
DME/Safety (Optional): Medical-grade incontinence pull-ups recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5 days to ensure the UTI has cleared and to discuss long-term urodynamic testing and management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if you develop a high fever (over 38.5 C), if you begin experiencing severe back pain just below your ribs, if you start vomiting continuously, or if you become completely unable to pass any urine at all.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The antibiotics will treat the acute infection, but managing the underlying chronic incontinence will require specialist testing later.
Clinical Course: The burning on your skin will improve immediately with the barrier cream. The constant urge to urinate should decrease within 48 hours of starting the antibiotics.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, severe back pain, or uncontrollable vomiting.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have developed a severe bladder infection, which has severely worsened your normal incontinence, causing your bladder to spasm and leak constantly. Because infected urine is very acidic, it has essentially caused chemical burns on your delicate skin. The antibiotics will kill the bladder bacteria, stopping the constant leaking. The thick cream acts as an immediate physical shield to stop the burning and let your skin heal.
Actionable Steps: Start the antibiotics tonight. Drink plenty of water. Apply the barrier cream thickly after gently patting the area dry, and wear the absorbent pull-ups provided.

